#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# exists

#% text
# `exists(x, formula)` is the statement that some `x` satisfies the formula; `exists([x, y], formula)` binds several variables, `exists(x, cond, formula)` is `exists(x, cond and formula)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `exists` stays as written; `resolve`, `reduce` and `cylindrical_decomposition` eliminate it.

#% item
# Variables that do not occur are dropped, and an `exists` over no variable is its formula.

#% item
# `not(exists(x, f))` is `for_all(x, not f)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An unresolved statement:

#% cell
exists(x, x^2 - x + 1 == 0)
#% output
#= exists(x, 1 - x + x^2 == 0)

#% text
# Resolved over the reals:

#% cell
resolve(exists(x, element(x, reals), x^2 - x + 1 == 0))
#% output
#= false

#% text
# A variable that does not occur:

#% cell
exists(x, y == 0)
#% output
#= y == 0

#% text
# The values of a for which a line meets an ellipse:

#% cell
reduce(exists([x, y], x^2 + a*y^2 <= 1 and x - y >= 2), a)
#% output
#= a <= 1/3

#% text
# Negation:

#% cell
not(exists(x, x == a))
#% output
#= for_all(x, x != a)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `resolve`, `for_all`, `cylindrical_decomposition`, `reduce`, `find_instance`
